  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1118 AM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nCloud cover moves further off to the NE Wednesday night into\nThursday as the upper low gets pulled further northeast into the\napproaching longwave trough axis and upper ridging builds a bit\nmore into the area. This should warm things up to near or slightly\nabove normal again with highs reaching the upper 80s to lower 90s \nacross northern Alabama and southern middle Tennessee. Heat index \nvalues look to climb back to around 100 degrees in some areas in \nthe afternoon. Overall though a break from clouds and rainfall is \nexpected through Thursday night.\n\nThis will change on Friday it seems, as a weak cold front\nassociated with a longwave trough axis aloft sinks southeast into\nthe area. Due to the weak nature of the front, only isolated to\nscattered showers or thunderstorm activity is expected (very weak\nforcing). This trough axis stalls through the weekend over the\narea. This should keep the heat from getting out of hand. Better \ninstability is expected to develop on Saturday along with better \nforcing along the front. This should increase shower and\nthunderstorm chances to between 30 and 60 percent in the\nafternoon. At this time shear looks too weak for any organized\nsevere thunderstorm activity. Highs should remain in the upper 80s\nto lower 90s with heat index values climb to just over 100 degrees\nin the early afternoon hours.",
